<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick William Koko</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as King Koko</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick William Koko was king of Nembe, also called Brass, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> delta of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, from 1889. A Christian who later returned to the traditional religion, he led the raid on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-niger-company">Royal Niger Company</a>'s depot at Akassa in 1895 in protest at the company's trade monopoly. After a British punitive expedition he withdrew into the interior, where he died in 1898.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-william-koko.jpg" alt="Frederick William Koko" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Signed (on prow of canoe) by Robert Barnes ARWS (1840–1895) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Koko_in_His_War_Canoe.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Koko was an Ijaw who had worked as a Christian schoolteacher before he became amanyanabo, or king. The leading chiefs of Nembe, among them Spiff, Samuel Sambo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, were Christians who had ordered the destruction of the Juju houses, and they chose him in 1889 to succeed King Ockiya largely because he shared their faith. The elderly Ebifa reigned alongside him as a second king at Bassambiri and was commander-in-chief until his death in 1894.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Nembe was a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> trade but poorer than its neighbours Bonny and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabar">Calabar</a>. From 1884 it lay within the British Oil Rivers Protectorate, and it refused a British treaty and opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-niger-company">Royal Niger Company</a>'s aim of controlling all trade on its rivers, which shut its traders out of markets they had long used. Koko sought alliances with Bonny and Okpoma, renounced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and, after Ebifa's death, struck at the company's headquarters at Akassa. His force arrived on 29 January 1895 in 22 war canoes, burnt the depot and carried prisoners back to Nembe, where many were killed the next day.</p><p>On 2 February Koko wrote to the British consul-general, Sir Claude MacDonald, that his quarrel was with the Niger Company and not with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, and he offered to release the hostages in return for free trade. The British refused. On 20 February a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> force under Rear Admiral Sir Frederick Bedford set out, and on 22 February it landed and burnt Nembe, destroying the houses of the king and his chiefs. A severe outbreak of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> killed many more of its people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Public opinion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> turned against the company and its director <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-taubman-goldie">George Goldie</a>, who was seen as having goaded Koko into war. The report of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-kirk">Sir John Kirk</a>, presented to Parliament in March 1896, found that 43 of Koko's prisoners had been murdered and eaten. In April 1896 Koko refused the settlement offered to him and was declared an outlaw and deposed, and a reward of £200 failed to bring him in. He died at Etiema, a remote village, in 1898, in a suspected suicide. The company lost its charter the next year, partly as a result of the war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick William Koko Mingi VIII of Nembe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Royal Niger Company shut Nembe traders out of the Niger markets, King <b>Frederick William Koko</b> led an attack on Akassa in 1895, and British forces burned Nembe in reprisal.</blockquote><a href="/state/nembe-kingdom">Nembe Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
